  }
});
---

<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Environment and nature: <em>trace the system before judging the response</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Environmental English becomes useful when separate words form a clear system. A speaker identifies a <span class="ex">problem</span>, names a supported <span class="ex">cause</span>, describes an <span class="ex">effect</span>, distinguishes strong evidence from possibility, and evaluates a <span class="ex">response</span> with its limits. Precision matters: <span class="ex">weather</span> is not <span class="ex">climate</span>, <span class="ex">emissions</span> are not automatically the same as <span class="ex">pollution</span>, and a proposal is not automatically a proven solution.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">system · evidence · cause · effect</span>
    <span class="tag">mitigation · adaptation · conservation</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Separate observation, explanation, effect, and response.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Distinguish</b><span>Organize climate, pollution, waste, habitats, and resources.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Evaluate</b><span>Match the strength of the language to the evidence.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Brief a fictional council on a response and its trade-offs.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can distinguish weather from climate; discuss greenhouse-gas emissions, pollution, waste, ecosystems, habitats, species, and biodiversity without collapsing them into one idea; build a specific cause-effect chain; report direct findings and cautious interpretations with different language; distinguish mitigation, adaptation, conservation, and restoration; compare individual and system-level action; evaluate a fictional proposal’s benefits, costs, and unanswered questions; and deliver a concise environmental briefing with intelligible word stress and connected speech.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered weather for today, a first conditional for a likely future consequence, and the passive when the process matters more than the actor.">
    <h3>Reactivate three useful foundations</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Environment foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="weather">weather</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will reduce">will reduce</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are separated">are separated</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="climate today">climate today</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A one-day forecast describes the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="weather" data-hint="Choose the word for short-term atmospheric conditions." data-fix="Today’s weather will be warm and windy.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The city expects this result: “If residents repair leaking faucets, they <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will reduce" data-hint="Use will plus the base verb for the expected result in this first conditional." data-fix="If residents repair leaking faucets, they will reduce water use.">choose</button> water use.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The process is the focus: “At this facility, glass and paper <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are separated" data-hint="Use the present passive because the materials receive the action." data-fix="At this facility, glass and paper are separated.">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice four different moves in one environmental account</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A fictional river update</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">“Water tests found unusually high nutrient levels downstream from several untreated discharge points.”</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">“The report says the discharge is contributing to excessive plant growth. As some of that plant matter breaks down, oxygen levels fall, which can harm fish.”</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">“The city has proposed inspecting the discharge points and upgrading the treatment system. It will publish new test results before deciding whether the measures are sufficient.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which line reports a measured observation?</li>
      <li>Which language connects a cause to an effect without claiming it is the only cause?</li>
      <li>Which response is proposed, and what evidence will be checked later?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the measured observation, contributing cause, possible biological effect, and proposed response.">
    <h3>Discovery check: observation, cause, effect, or response?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Environmental account choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="high nutrient levels">high nutrient levels</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="untreated discharge">untreated discharge</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="harm to fish">harm to fish</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="upgrading the treatment system">upgrading the treatment system</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The measured observation is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="high nutrient levels" data-hint="Look for what the water tests directly found." data-fix="The tests found high nutrient levels downstream.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The report identifies <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="untreated discharge" data-hint="Find the human activity described as contributing to the problem." data-fix="The report identifies untreated discharge as a contributing cause.">choose</button> as a contributing cause.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The possible effect on wildlife is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="harm to fish" data-hint="Find what lower oxygen can do to living organisms." data-fix="Lower oxygen can cause harm to fish.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>One proposed system response is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="upgrading the treatment system" data-hint="Choose the planned infrastructure change, not the observed problem." data-fix="The city has proposed upgrading the treatment system.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: time scale and material type change the noun</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>core meaning</th><th>natural example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">weather</span></td><td>short-term conditions in the atmosphere</td><td><span class="ex">Today’s weather is hot and humid.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">climate</span></td><td>typical patterns and variation measured across a long period</td><td><span class="ex">The region has a dry climate.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">pollution</span></td><td>harmful contamination of air, water, or land; normally uncountable</td><td><span class="ex">Traffic contributes to local air pollution.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">waste</span></td><td>material that is discarded or no longer wanted; normally uncountable here</td><td><span class="ex">The cafeteria produces less food waste now.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">litter</span></td><td>waste left in the wrong public place</td><td><span class="ex">Volunteers removed litter from the riverbank.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A single hot afternoon is evidence about the day’s weather, not enough evidence by itself to establish or reject a long-term climate trend. A heat wave can be studied as part of a larger pattern, but the speaker should name the time scale and source. Likewise, <span class="ex">waste</span> is not automatically <span class="ex">pollution</span>. Waste becomes pollution when it contaminates or harms the environment.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used weather for the afternoon, climate for a thirty-year pattern, pollution for contaminated air, waste for discarded material, and litter for material left in a public place.">
    <h3>Choose the exact scale or material relationship</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Weather climate pollution waste litter choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="weather">weather</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="climate">climate</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="pollution">pollution</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="waste">waste</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="litter">litter</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The forecast is only for this afternoon, so it describes the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="weather" data-hint="An afternoon is a short time scale." data-fix="The forecast describes this afternoon’s weather.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The graph summarizes temperature and rainfall patterns over thirty years, so it describes the regional <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="climate" data-hint="A thirty-year pattern belongs to the long-term scale." data-fix="The graph describes the regional climate.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Measurements found harmful particles in the neighborhood’s air. The report calls this air <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="pollution" data-hint="The particles are contaminating the air." data-fix="The report calls this air pollution.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The factory is redesigning its packaging so it discards less material. Its goal is to reduce packaging <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="waste" data-hint="Choose the uncountable noun for discarded material." data-fix="Its goal is to reduce packaging waste.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Cups and wrappers were left on the public beach instead of placed in a bin. Volunteers collected the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="litter" data-hint="Choose the word for waste left in the wrong public place." data-fix="Volunteers collected the litter from the beach.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Separate greenhouse-gas emissions from local air pollution</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">An emission</span> is something released into the air. <span class="ex">Greenhouse gases</span>, including carbon dioxide and methane, trap heat in the atmosphere. Burning <span class="ex">fossil fuels</span> can release greenhouse gases and can also release substances that contribute to local air pollution. The categories can overlap, but they are not identical: a speaker should name the substance or effect when the information provides it.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Renewable energy</span> comes from sources that are naturally replenished, such as sunlight and wind. That does not mean every project has zero impact. A careful evaluation can compare emissions, land use, cost, reliability, materials, and effects on nearby communities or habitats.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished a released substance, heat-trapping gases, fossil fuels, renewable energy, and local air pollution rather than treating them as synonyms.">
    <h3>Classify the relationship described in the evidence</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Emissions and energy choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="emission">emission</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="greenhouse gases">greenhouse gases</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="fossil fuels">fossil fuels</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="renewable energy">renewable energy</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="air pollution">air pollution</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The report measures a substance released from a smokestack. Each release is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="emission" data-hint="Choose the countable noun for something released into the air." data-fix="Each measured release is an emission.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Carbon dioxide and methane are grouped together because they trap heat. They are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="greenhouse gases" data-hint="Choose the category defined by its heat-trapping effect." data-fix="Carbon dioxide and methane are greenhouse gases.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Coal, oil, and natural gas formed over very long periods and are burned for energy. They are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="fossil fuels" data-hint="Choose the established category for coal, oil, and natural gas." data-fix="Coal, oil, and natural gas are fossil fuels.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The project generates power from sunlight, a source that is naturally replenished. It produces <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="renewable energy" data-hint="Choose the energy category based on a replenished source." data-fix="The solar project produces renewable energy.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Monitors beside the highway found harmful concentrations of fine particles in the surrounding air. The finding concerns local <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="air pollution" data-hint="The evidence concerns harmful contamination of local air." data-fix="The finding concerns local air pollution.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Map living systems: habitat, species, wildlife, ecosystem, and biodiversity</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>relationship</th><th>usage note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">habitat</span></td><td>the natural home or environment of an organism</td><td>countable: <span class="ex">a wetland habitat</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">species</span></td><td>a biological group whose members share key characteristics</td><td>same form in singular and plural: <span class="ex">one species, several species</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">wildlife</span></td><td>wild animals, plants, and other organisms collectively</td><td>normally uncountable: <span class="ex">protect wildlife</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">ecosystem</span></td><td>organisms and the physical environment interacting as a system</td><td>countable: <span class="ex">a coastal ecosystem</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">biodiversity</span></td><td>the variety of living things at different biological levels</td><td>normally uncountable: <span class="ex">support biodiversity</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Deforestation</span> is the large-scale clearing or loss of forests. It can cause <span class="ex">habitat loss</span>, but the exact effects depend on the place, scale, species, and later land use. <span class="ex">Conservation</span> protects species, habitats, and resources; <span class="ex">restoration</span> helps a damaged ecosystem recover.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified a natural home, a biological group, wild organisms collectively, an interacting system, and variety across living things.">
    <h3>Choose the ecological relationship, not only a nature word</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Ecology choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="habitat">habitat</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="species">species</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="wildlife">wildlife</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="ecosystem">ecosystem</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="biodiversity">biodiversity</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The marsh supplies nesting space and food for the bird. It is the bird’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="habitat" data-hint="Choose the countable noun for the organism’s natural home." data-fix="The marsh is the bird’s habitat.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The survey found one frog group that scientists had not recorded in this valley before. It may be a newly recorded <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="species" data-hint="The same noun form is used for one or several biological groups." data-fix="It may be a newly recorded species in this valley.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The rule protects the area’s wild animals and plants collectively. It protects <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="wildlife" data-hint="Choose the normally uncountable collective noun." data-fix="The rule protects local wildlife.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The study examines fish, plants, water, soil, and their interactions in the lake. It examines the lake <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="ecosystem" data-hint="Choose the word for organisms and their physical environment interacting." data-fix="It examines the lake ecosystem.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The reserve contains many kinds of plants, insects, birds, and genetic variation within populations. It supports <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="biodiversity" data-hint="Choose the uncountable noun for variety across living things." data-fix="The reserve supports biodiversity.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Match the strength of the claim to the strength of the evidence</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Direct findings can use <span class="ex">the monitors recorded</span>, <span class="ex">the survey found</span>, or <span class="ex">the data show</span>. An interpretation with uncertainty can use <span class="ex">suggests, may, might, could</span>, or <span class="ex">is consistent with</span>. A supported causal account can use <span class="ex">causes, leads to, results in</span>, or <span class="ex">contributes to</span>. Use <span class="ex">contributes to</span> when the source identifies one factor among several.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Affects</span> takes a direct object: <span class="ex">Pollution affects wildlife</span>, not <span class="ex">affects to wildlife</span>. <span class="ex">Results from</span> points backward to a cause; <span class="ex">results in</span> points forward to an effect. Do not change <span class="ex">is associated with</span> into <span class="ex">causes</span> unless the evidence supports causation.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You reported a direct measurement, marked a cautious interpretation, named a contributing cause, pointed from effect to cause with results from, and used affect without to.">
    <h3>Choose an evidence relationship the source can defend</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Evidence relationship choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="recorded">recorded</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="may">may</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="contributes to">contributes to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="results from">results from</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="affects">affects</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The monitor directly measured 42 micrograms per cubic meter at 8 a.m. It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="recorded" data-hint="Choose the reporting verb for a direct instrument reading." data-fix="The monitor recorded 42 micrograms per cubic meter at 8 a.m.">choose</button> that reading.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The team has one small preliminary sample, so the report says the new pattern <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="may" data-hint="Choose the modal that keeps the interpretation appropriately cautious." data-fix="The report says the new pattern may require further study.">choose</button> require further study.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The source names traffic as one of several causes of poor air quality. Traffic <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="contributes to" data-hint="Choose the phrase that identifies one factor without claiming it is the only cause." data-fix="Traffic contributes to poor air quality.">choose</button> poor air quality.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The explanation starts with the effect, soil erosion, and then names the cause, loss of plant cover. Soil erosion <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="results from" data-hint="Results from points from an effect back to its cause." data-fix="Soil erosion results from the loss of plant cover.">choose</button> the loss of plant cover.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The verb takes wildlife directly as its object: “Water pollution <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="affects" data-hint="Affect takes a direct object without to." data-fix="Water pollution affects wildlife.">choose</button> wildlife.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Classify responses by what they change</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>response</th><th>core purpose</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">mitigation</span></td><td>reduce a cause or limit the size of climate change</td><td><span class="ex">Improve efficiency and reduce greenhouse-gas emissions.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">adaptation</span></td><td>prepare for or adjust to effects that are happening or expected</td><td><span class="ex">Create shaded cooling areas during extreme heat.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">conservation</span></td><td>protect species, habitats, or resources</td><td><span class="ex">Protect a wetland from development.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">restoration</span></td><td>help a damaged ecosystem recover</td><td><span class="ex">Replant native vegetation along a damaged riverbank.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">waste prevention</span></td><td>avoid creating waste before reuse or recycling is needed</td><td><span class="ex">Replace disposable cups with a refill system.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Reduce, reuse, recycle</span> is useful, but it is a hierarchy only when the context supports the choices. Avoiding unnecessary material often prevents more waste than processing the same material later. Individual action can matter, while organizations and public agencies can change infrastructure, product design, rules, and access. A careful speaker evaluates who has the power to act, what the response costs, who benefits, and what new problem it might create.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You classified cutting a cause as mitigation, heat preparation as adaptation, protection as conservation, ecosystem repair as restoration, and avoiding disposables as waste prevention.">
    <h3>Classify the response by its primary purpose</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Environmental response choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="mitigation">mitigation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="adaptation">adaptation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="conservation">conservation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="restoration">restoration</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="waste prevention">waste prevention</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A transit agency replaces inefficient diesel buses to reduce greenhouse-gas emissions. Its primary climate purpose is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="mitigation" data-hint="The action targets a cause by reducing emissions." data-fix="Replacing inefficient buses to cut emissions is mitigation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A city opens cooling centers because more extreme heat is expected. This preparation is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="adaptation" data-hint="The city is preparing for an expected effect." data-fix="Opening cooling centers for expected extreme heat is adaptation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A land agreement prevents construction in an undamaged nesting area. Its primary purpose is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="conservation" data-hint="The agreement protects an existing habitat." data-fix="Protecting the undamaged nesting area is conservation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>A project removes invasive plants and replants native vegetation in a damaged marsh. Its primary purpose is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="restoration" data-hint="The project is helping a damaged ecosystem recover." data-fix="Repairing the damaged marsh is restoration.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A café introduces durable cups so disposable cups are never created. This is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="waste prevention" data-hint="The action avoids creating the discarded item in the first place." data-fix="Replacing disposable cups with durable cups is waste prevention.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: Make the key contrasts audible</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Keep the stressed syllable clear: <span class="ex">CLI-mate</span> /ˈklaɪmət/, <span class="ex">HAB-i-tat</span> /ˈhæbəˌtæt/, <span class="ex">pol-LU-tion</span> /pəˈluʃən/, <span class="ex">re-NEW-a-ble</span> /rɪˈnuəbəl/, and <span class="ex">e-MIS-sions</span> /ɪˈmɪʃənz/. <span class="ex">Species</span> has two syllables in common U.S. speech: /ˈspiʃiz/. In connected speech, link the final consonant into the next vowel in <span class="ex">result_in</span> and <span class="ex">protect_our habitat</span>. Clarity matters more than copying one accent.</p>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard the stressed syllable and the cause-effect relationship rather than relying on one environmental keyword.">
    <h3>Tutor-read listening: hear the word and the relationship</h3>
    <p class="note-line"><b>Tutor:</b> Read each line once naturally, then once more if requested. <b>Learner:</b> Choose the meaning or sound pattern you heard.</p>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Today’s conditions are temporary, but the long-term climate is getting drier." data-hint="Listen for the contrast between today and a long-term pattern." data-fix="Today’s conditions are temporary, but the long-term climate is getting drier."><p class="quiz-question">1. Tutor line: “Today’s weather is wet, although the regional climate has become drier over several decades.” Which paraphrase preserves the time-scale contrast?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Today’s conditions are temporary, but the long-term climate is getting drier.">Today’s conditions are temporary, but the long-term climate is getting drier.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="One wet day proves the long-term climate is becoming wetter.">One wet day proves the long-term climate is becoming wetter.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Weather and climate always refer to the same time scale.">Weather and climate always refer to the same time scale.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The second syllable is stressed: pol-LU-tion." data-hint="Listen for the strongest beat in the middle of the word." data-fix="The second syllable is stressed: pol-LU-tion."><p class="quiz-question">2. Tutor line: “The monitors recorded air pollution near the highway.” Which stress pattern did you hear in <span class="ex">pollution</span>?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The second syllable is stressed: pol-LU-tion.">The second syllable is stressed: pol-LU-tion.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The first syllable is stressed: POL-lu-tion.">The first syllable is stressed: POL-lu-tion.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every syllable receives equal stress.">Every syllable receives equal stress.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Traffic is one factor among several that worsen the air." data-hint="Contributes to does not claim that traffic is the only cause." data-fix="Traffic is one factor among several that worsen the air."><p class="quiz-question">3. Tutor line: “Heavy traffic contributes to poor air quality downtown.” What relationship did you hear?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Traffic is one factor among several that worsen the air.">Traffic is one factor among several that worsen the air.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Traffic is proven to be the only cause of every air-quality problem.">Traffic is proven to be the only cause of every air-quality problem.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Poor air quality causes all downtown traffic.">Poor air quality causes all downtown traffic.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The action prepares the city for an expected effect." data-hint="Listen for preparing for extreme heat rather than reducing its cause." data-fix="The action prepares the city for an expected effect."><p class="quiz-question">4. Tutor line: “The city is adding shaded bus stops to adapt to more frequent extreme heat.” What is the action’s primary purpose?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The action prepares the city for an expected effect.">The action prepares the city for an expected effect.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The action directly eliminates every cause of climate change.">The action directly eliminates every cause of climate change.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The action measures biodiversity in a wetland.">The action measures biodiversity in a wetland.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Protect the wetland because it is the species’ natural home." data-hint="Habitat names a natural home; species names the biological group." data-fix="Protect the wetland because it is the species’ natural home."><p class="quiz-question">5. Tutor line: “Protecting this wetland habitat could help the local frog species.” Which meaning is accurate?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Protect the wetland because it is the species’ natural home.">Protect the wetland because it is the species’ natural home.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Protect the species because it is the wetland’s weather forecast.">Protect the species because it is the wetland’s weather forecast.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Habitat and species both mean discarded material.">Habitat and species both mean discarded material.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
