  <h2>Read a proposal as evidence, mechanism, and trade-off</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Arroyo City heat brief</h3>
    <p><b>Observed problem:</b> During a six-week study last summer, afternoon surface temperatures at three treeless bus stops averaged 7°F higher than at three nearby stops with mature shade trees. The brief does not claim that this small comparison represents every neighborhood.</p>
    <p><b>Proposal:</b> The transit agency proposes adding shade structures at twelve high-use stops and planting native trees where underground utilities and available water make planting practical.</p>
    <p><b>Expected mechanism:</b> Shade could lower the amount of solar energy that reaches people and paved surfaces. Trees may provide additional shade as they mature.</p>
    <p><b>Trade-offs and evaluation:</b> Structures have an initial cost and require maintenance. Young trees require water and time to grow. The agency will record shaded and unshaded temperatures, water use, maintenance costs, and rider feedback for two summers before expanding the program.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished a limited observation, an adaptation mechanism, a supplied constraint, the planned evaluation, and a claim that remains cautious.">
    <h3>Evidence check: what can this fictional brief defend?</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The six studied treeless stops were hotter on average than the six studied shaded stops during the study period." data-hint="Keep the claim limited to the measured stops and six-week period." data-fix="The six studied treeless stops were hotter on average than the six studied shaded stops during the study period."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which statement reports the observation without overgeneralizing?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The six studied treeless stops were hotter on average than the six studied shaded stops during the study period.">The six studied treeless stops were hotter on average than the six studied shaded stops during the study period.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every treeless place in every city is exactly 7°F hotter forever.">Every treeless place in every city is exactly 7°F hotter forever.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The study proved that buses create all extreme heat.">The study proved that buses create all extreme heat.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The project primarily adapts public waiting areas to an expected heat effect." data-hint="The proposal changes exposure to heat at bus stops rather than directly targeting the global cause." data-fix="The project primarily adapts public waiting areas to an expected heat effect."><p class="quiz-question">2. What is the proposal’s primary environmental response type?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The project primarily adapts public waiting areas to an expected heat effect.">The project primarily adapts public waiting areas to an expected heat effect.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The project restores a damaged ocean ecosystem.">The project restores a damaged ocean ecosystem.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The project eliminates all greenhouse-gas emissions worldwide.">The project eliminates all greenhouse-gas emissions worldwide.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Young trees require water and time to mature." data-hint="Choose the practical limit stated directly in the brief." data-fix="Young trees require water and time to mature."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which tree-related trade-off does the brief explicitly identify?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Young trees require water and time to mature.">Young trees require water and time to mature.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every native tree immediately provides complete shade without maintenance.">Every native tree immediately provides complete shade without maintenance.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Trees turn renewable energy into litter.">Trees turn renewable energy into litter.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Compare temperatures, water use, maintenance costs, and rider feedback over two summers." data-hint="Find the four measures and the stated evaluation period." data-fix="The agency will compare temperatures, water use, maintenance costs, and rider feedback over two summers."><p class="quiz-question">4. How will the agency evaluate whether expansion is justified?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Compare temperatures, water use, maintenance costs, and rider feedback over two summers.">Compare temperatures, water use, maintenance costs, and rider feedback over two summers.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Assume success before installing anything and collect no evidence.">Assume success before installing anything and collect no evidence.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Measure only the spelling of the word climate.">Measure only the spelling of the word climate.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Shade could reduce heat exposure at the selected stops, but the pilot evidence is not yet complete." data-hint="Use could for the expected mechanism and keep the conclusion open until the pilot is evaluated." data-fix="Shade could reduce heat exposure at the selected stops, but the pilot evidence is not yet complete."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which conclusion best matches the brief’s current evidence?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Shade could reduce heat exposure at the selected stops, but the pilot evidence is not yet complete.">Shade could reduce heat exposure at the selected stops, but the pilot evidence is not yet complete.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The proposal is guaranteed to solve every climate and transit problem at no cost.">The proposal is guaranteed to solve every climate and transit problem at no cost.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The small study proves shade can never influence surface temperature.">The small study proves shade can never influence surface temperature.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build one cause-effect chain and one cautious recommendation</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="loss of plant cover can lead to soil erosion" data-hint="Begin with loss of plant cover, then add can lead to and the effect." data-fix="Loss of plant cover can lead to soil erosion." data-success-message="Correct. Can marks possibility, and lead to points from the supplied cause to the effect.">
    <h3>Build 1: connect a cause to a possible effect</h3><p class="tile-target">Use the supplied relationship without claiming that erosion always occurs.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="soil">soil</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="lead">lead</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="plant">plant</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="erosion">erosion</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="loss">Loss</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="cover">cover</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="to">to</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="of">of</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="can">can</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="the city should test the proposal before expanding it" data-hint="Begin with the city should test the proposal, then add before expanding it." data-fix="The city should test the proposal before expanding it." data-success-message="Correct. The recommendation requests evidence from a test before a larger commitment.">
    <h3>Build 2: recommend an evidence step</h3><p class="tile-target">Ask for a pilot before citywide expansion.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="expanding">expanding</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="proposal">proposal</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">The</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="it">it</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="test">test</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="city">city</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="before">before</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="should">should</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice and repair the relationship</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="climate" data-hint="The sentence describes conditions during one afternoon." data-fix="The weather this afternoon will be cloudy with heavy rain." data-why="An afternoon forecast describes short-term weather, not a long-term climate pattern." data-success-message="Correct. Weather accurately names the short-term forecast.">
    <h3>Repair 1: choose the time scale</h3><p>Tap the noun that incorrectly describes an afternoon forecast.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="the">The</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="climate">climate</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="afternoon">this afternoon</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="rain">will be cloudy with heavy rain.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="to" data-hint="Affect takes its object directly." data-fix="Plastic pollution affects marine wildlife." data-why="Affect is a transitive verb here, so marine wildlife follows without to." data-success-message="Correct. Affects now takes marine wildlife directly as its object.">
    <h3>Repair 2: remove the extra preposition</h3><p>Tap the word that breaks the verb-object relationship.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="plastic">Plastic pollution</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="affects">affects</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="to">to</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="wildlife">marine wildlife.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="in" data-hint="The sentence starts with the effect and then points backward to its cause." data-fix="The decline in oxygen results from excessive plant growth and decomposition." data-why="Results from introduces a cause; results in introduces the effect that follows a cause." data-success-message="Correct. Results from now points backward from the decline to its cause.">
    <h3>Repair 3: point in the correct direction</h3><p>Tap the preposition that incorrectly introduces the cause.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="decline">The decline in oxygen</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="results">results</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="in">in</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="growth">excessive plant growth and decomposition.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Practice a public issue without disclosing private information</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to reveal a real home address, utility bill, health condition, immigration status, workplace practice, political affiliation, financial situation, or conflict with a neighbor, employer, landlord, or public agency. Use fictional Arroyo City and the supplied evidence. A real local example is optional and should never be required to practice environmental language.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>System map:</b> Identify one observed problem, one supported cause, one effect, and one group with the power to respond.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Evidence ladder:</b> Mark each statement as a direct finding, interpretation, causal claim, or unanswered question. Adjust the certainty language.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Response comparison:</b> Compare one individual action and one organization or city action. Explain their different reach and limits without blaming a person.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Trade-off check:</b> Ask who benefits, who pays, what resource is needed, what could go wrong, and how success will be measured.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: deliver a 90-second fictional council briefing</h3>
    <p>Use the Arroyo City brief or a tutor-supplied fictional issue. Define the problem at the correct time and geographic scale; report one direct observation; name a supported cause and effect; use one cautious interpretation; classify a proposed response as mitigation, adaptation, conservation, restoration, or waste prevention; compare one benefit with one trade-off; recommend one measurable next step; and answer one review-panel question. Use at least six target chunks and make the stress clear in <span class="ex">climate, habitat, pollution, renewable</span>, and <span class="ex">emissions</span>.</p>
    <p><b>Evidence check:</b> Can the listener tell what was measured, what is inferred, what causes what, which ecological relationship each noun names, how the response works, what limitation remains, and what evidence would justify the next decision?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved the weather-climate time scale, separated emissions from pollution, used species accurately, matched claim strength to evidence, classified adaptation, and selected an evaluable recommendation.">
    <h3>Final check: choose the account the evidence can defend</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Today is unusually cool, although the thirty-year record shows a warmer average climate." data-hint="Keep today’s weather separate from the long-term record." data-fix="Today is unusually cool, although the thirty-year record shows a warmer average climate."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which sentence accurately contrasts a one-day observation with a climate pattern?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Today is unusually cool, although the thirty-year record shows a warmer average climate.">Today is unusually cool, although the thirty-year record shows a warmer average climate.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Today is cool, so the thirty-year climate trend cannot exist.">Today is cool, so the thirty-year climate trend cannot exist.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="One afternoon and thirty years are the same environmental time scale.">One afternoon and thirty years are the same environmental time scale.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The inventory measured carbon dioxide emissions, while roadside monitors measured harmful local air pollution." data-hint="Choose the sentence that keeps the released greenhouse gas and harmful local contamination distinct." data-fix="The inventory measured carbon dioxide emissions, while roadside monitors measured harmful local air pollution."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which report distinguishes emissions from local air pollution?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The inventory measured carbon dioxide emissions, while roadside monitors measured harmful local air pollution.">The inventory measured carbon dioxide emissions, while roadside monitors measured harmful local air pollution.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every emission is automatically identical to every form of pollution.">Every emission is automatically identical to every form of pollution.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Air pollution and renewable energy always mean the same thing.">Air pollution and renewable energy always mean the same thing.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The survey recorded one species in the north marsh and three species in the south marsh." data-hint="Species has the same form after one and after three." data-fix="The survey recorded one species in the north marsh and three species in the south marsh."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which sentence uses the singular and plural of <span class="ex">species</span> accurately?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The survey recorded one species in the north marsh and three species in the south marsh.">The survey recorded one species in the north marsh and three species in the south marsh.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The survey recorded one specie and three specieses.">The survey recorded one specie and three specieses.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The survey recorded a wildlife and three biodiversities.">The survey recorded a wildlife and three biodiversities.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The small sample suggests a possible pattern that should be tested with more data." data-hint="The source is preliminary, so choose cautious language and a next evidence step." data-fix="The small sample suggests a possible pattern that should be tested with more data."><p class="quiz-question">4. A study has one small preliminary sample. Which conclusion matches the evidence strength?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The small sample suggests a possible pattern that should be tested with more data.">The small sample suggests a possible pattern that should be tested with more data.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The small sample proves the pattern is universal and permanent.">The small sample proves the pattern is universal and permanent.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="No observation can ever suggest a question for further study.">No observation can ever suggest a question for further study.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Adding cooling centers prepares residents for extreme heat, so its primary purpose is adaptation." data-hint="The action prepares people for an effect rather than directly reducing its cause." data-fix="Adding cooling centers prepares residents for extreme heat, so its primary purpose is adaptation."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which sentence classifies a response by its primary purpose?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Adding cooling centers prepares residents for extreme heat, so its primary purpose is adaptation.">Adding cooling centers prepares residents for extreme heat, so its primary purpose is adaptation.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Adding cooling centers restores every damaged wetland, so it is restoration.">Adding cooling centers restores every damaged wetland, so it is restoration.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Adding cooling centers prevents all packaging waste, so it is recycling.">Adding cooling centers prevents all packaging waste, so it is recycling.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Pilot the shade plan, compare temperatures and costs for two summers, then decide whether to expand it." data-hint="Choose the recommendation with a test, relevant measures, a time period, and a later decision." data-fix="Pilot the shade plan, compare temperatures and costs for two summers, then decide whether to expand it."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which recommendation creates evidence for a later decision?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pilot the shade plan, compare temperatures and costs for two summers, then decide whether to expand it.">Pilot the shade plan, compare temperatures and costs for two summers, then decide whether to expand it.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Expand the plan everywhere immediately and never measure any result or cost.">Expand the plan everywhere immediately and never measure any result or cost.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Reject every response because no policy can ever be evaluated.">Reject every response because no policy can ever be evaluated.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
